1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,980
Xiao Yue, when you say the cake is almost ready, is it ready

2
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:05,565
or not?

3
00:00:05,565 --> 00:00:10,069
Close the oven door first, or it really will never be ready.

4
00:00:10,069 --> 00:00:14,146
But the phrase chà bu duō makes me feel very insecure.

5
00:00:14,146 --> 00:00:16,166
Why?

6
00:00:16,166 --> 00:00:18,686
Yesterday my friend said he was almost there,

7
00:00:18,686 --> 00:00:22,028
and I waited another twenty-five minutes at the door.

8
00:00:22,028 --> 00:00:25,692
So today you've come to settle the score with chà bu duō.

9
00:00:25,692 --> 00:00:28,925
Exactly. I want to know how big the difference can be.

10
00:00:28,925 --> 00:00:33,430
Here's the short answer: chà bu duō doesn't have a fixed number.

11
00:00:33,430 --> 00:00:35,210
That's the problem!

12
00:00:35,210 --> 00:00:40,024
Can one minute and half an hour both count as chà bu duō?

13
00:00:40,024 --> 00:00:43,257
It depends on the situation and on the person speaking.

14
00:00:43,257 --> 00:00:46,421
Then let's start with the simplest meaning.

15
00:00:46,421 --> 00:00:52,570
The most common meaning is that two things are close, but not exactly the same.

16
00:00:52,570 --> 00:00:55,153
For example, these two cakes are about the same.

17
00:00:55,153 --> 00:01:00,589
Right. Their size, taste, or price might be very similar.

18
00:01:00,589 --> 00:01:04,629
But if one is chocolate and one is strawberry,

19
00:01:04,629 --> 00:01:06,234
can I still say that?

20
00:01:06,234 --> 00:01:09,514
If you're only saying their sizes are similar,

21
00:01:09,514 --> 00:01:12,597
yes. If you mean their taste, no.

22
00:01:12,597 --> 00:01:15,758
So it's best to say in what way they're similar.

23
00:01:15,758 --> 00:01:20,976
Exactly. The more specific you are, the less likely people are to misunderstand.

24
00:01:20,976 --> 00:01:23,956
It's natural to say Sophie and I look similar,

25
00:01:23,956 --> 00:01:25,479
right?

26
00:01:25,479 --> 00:01:29,499
Yes. It means you two look alike.

27
00:01:29,499 --> 00:01:34,435
Actually, only our eyes look a little alike. Everything else is quite different.

28
00:01:34,435 --> 00:01:38,602
Then you can say, "Our eyes look similar."

29
00:01:38,602 --> 00:01:41,757
What's the difference between chà bu duō and yí yàng, "the same"?

30
00:01:41,757 --> 00:01:48,127
Yí yàng means there's no obvious difference. Chà bu duō means there is still a slight difference.

31
00:01:48,127 --> 00:01:51,282
Both coffees are twenty yuan, so the prices are the same.

32
00:01:51,282 --> 00:01:56,430
If one is twenty and one is twenty-two, you can say the prices are about the same.

33
00:01:56,430 --> 00:01:59,364
What if one is twenty and one is thirty-five?

34
00:01:59,364 --> 00:02:02,153
I wouldn't call that about the same.

35
00:02:02,153 --> 00:02:04,801
Maybe a rich person would?

36
00:02:04,801 --> 00:02:09,183
See? Chà bu duō is also affected by personal judgment.

37
00:02:09,183 --> 00:02:12,900
So it isn't a math word. It's a feeling word.

38
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:14,860
Well said.

39
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:19,836
In everyday speech, it often means roughly within the same range.

40
00:02:19,836 --> 00:02:22,410
What does "range" mean?

41
00:02:22,410 --> 00:02:26,772
It's the part between one number and another.

42
00:02:26,772 --> 00:02:30,073
For example, twenty to twenty-five yuan is a range.

43
00:02:30,073 --> 00:02:35,301
Right. Within that range, we might feel the prices are similar.

44
00:02:35,301 --> 00:02:38,946
Then 178 and 180 centimeters are also about the same height.

45
00:02:38,946 --> 00:02:42,095
Yes, because there's only a two-centimeter difference.

46
00:02:42,095 --> 00:02:44,177
What about 160 and 180 centimeters?

47
00:02:44,177 --> 00:02:49,414
Normally you wouldn't say they're about the same, unless you're comparing very roughly.

48
00:02:49,414 --> 00:02:53,494
Comparing roughly means not looking at the details?

49
00:02:53,494 --> 00:02:57,640
Right. You only look at the general picture and don't demand great accuracy.

50
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:02,302
The English word "almost" is also often translated as chà bu duō, right?

51
00:03:02,302 --> 00:03:05,875
Sometimes, but you can't swap them every time.

52
00:03:05,875 --> 00:03:07,895
Why not?

53
00:03:07,895 --> 00:03:12,395
Because "almost" often means something isn't finished yet, but is close to being

54
00:03:12,395 --> 00:03:14,347
finished.

55
00:03:14,347 --> 00:03:16,511
Like "The cake is almost ready."

56
00:03:16,511 --> 00:03:21,946
Right. It needs a little more time, but it's very close to being done.

57
00:03:21,946 --> 00:03:25,377
Then "I'm almost there" also means I haven't arrived, but I'm close.

58
00:03:25,377 --> 00:03:28,026
Usually, yes.

59
00:03:28,026 --> 00:03:30,762
Usually? Now I'm uncertain again.

60
00:03:30,762 --> 00:03:35,931
Haha. Some people say "I'm almost there" even when they've only reached the neighborhood.

61
00:03:35,931 --> 00:03:39,865
No wonder I never know when to go downstairs.

62
00:03:39,865 --> 00:03:42,724
You can ask, "How many more minutes?"

63
00:03:42,724 --> 00:03:45,600
That's more useful than asking, "Are you here yet?"

64
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:50,477
Right. When you need to plan your time, a specific number is clearer.

65
00:03:50,477 --> 00:03:54,637
If I say, "I'm almost finished eating," does that mean there's still a little food on my plate?

66
00:03:54,637 --> 00:03:59,073
There may be a few bites left, or you may just be slowly wrapping up.

67
00:03:59,073 --> 00:04:01,883
"Wrapping up" means doing the last little bit.

68
00:04:01,883 --> 00:04:06,803
Exactly. "I'm almost done writing" and "I'm almost ready" have that same

69
00:04:06,803 --> 00:04:08,246
feeling.

70
00:04:08,246 --> 00:04:13,183
If I've already finished writing, can I still say, "I'm almost done"?

71
00:04:13,183 --> 00:04:14,943
Usually not.

72
00:04:14,943 --> 00:04:18,480
If it's finished, just say you're done.

73
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:21,415
Unless I still want to check it.

74
00:04:21,415 --> 00:04:25,933
Then you can say, "The content is almost finished. I still need to check it."

75
00:04:25,933 --> 00:04:30,808
So chà bu duō lets you leave yourself a little room.

76
00:04:30,808 --> 00:04:35,543
Yes, but it may also leave other people unsure about how far you've actually gotten.

77
00:04:35,543 --> 00:04:40,264
If my boss asks whether the work is finished and I say, "Almost," is that dangerous?

78
00:04:40,264 --> 00:04:45,420
A little. Your boss's next question will probably be, "How much is left?"

79
00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:47,440
Then what should I say?

80
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:53,238
For example, "The main part is done. I can finish checking it by three this afternoon."

81
00:04:53,238 --> 00:04:56,190
That gives both the progress and the completion time.

82
00:04:56,190 --> 00:05:02,123
Right. At work, people need to coordinate, so specific information is more responsible than chà bu duō.

83
00:05:02,123 --> 00:05:07,915
But in a casual conversation, can being too exact sound strange?

84
00:05:07,915 --> 00:05:14,235
It can. If a friend asks when you went to bed and you say 10:47,

85
00:05:14,235 --> 00:05:16,066
that sounds a bit too serious.

86
00:05:16,066 --> 00:05:18,428
I can say, "Around eleven."

87
00:05:18,428 --> 00:05:19,848
Very natural.

88
00:05:19,848 --> 00:05:23,009
Here chà bu duō means "approximately."

89
00:05:23,009 --> 00:05:26,724
Are "chà bu duō eleven" and "eleven-ish" the same?

90
00:05:26,724 --> 00:05:31,740
They're very close. Both mean it could be a little before or after.

91
00:05:31,740 --> 00:05:33,760
What about "probably eleven"?

92
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:35,780
About the same.

93
00:05:35,780 --> 00:05:37,800
You did that on purpose, didn't you?

94
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:41,100
I'm giving you a live demonstration.

95
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:44,176
Are there subtle differences among those three expressions?

96
00:05:44,176 --> 00:05:50,706
Zuǒ yòu usually comes after a time or number, like "around eleven"

97
00:05:50,706 --> 00:05:52,253
or "around five people."

98
00:05:52,253 --> 00:05:55,543
Dà gài can go before it: "probably eleven."

99
00:05:55,543 --> 00:06:00,043
Right. Chà bu duō is more flexible. You can say "around eleven,"

100
00:06:00,043 --> 00:06:02,194
or "It's about time."

101
00:06:02,194 --> 00:06:05,200
What does "It's about time" mean here?

102
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:09,222
It means the time has come to do the next thing.

103
00:06:09,222 --> 00:06:12,794
For example, after we've talked for a long time, you say, "It's about time."

104
00:06:12,794 --> 00:06:17,659
That means it's time to end or move on to the next topic.

105
00:06:17,659 --> 00:06:20,817
It doesn't give an exact time, but everyone understands.

106
00:06:20,817 --> 00:06:23,894
Because we know the situation at that moment.

107
00:06:23,894 --> 00:06:28,690
Chinese really likes putting important information into the context.

108
00:06:28,690 --> 00:06:32,848
All languages do that, actually. They just do it in different ways.

109
00:06:32,848 --> 00:06:38,014
My grandma never watches the clock when she cooks. She always says, "Close enough is fine."

110
00:06:38,014 --> 00:06:40,814
That's another common meaning of chà bu duō:

111
00:06:40,814 --> 00:06:43,610
meeting the basic requirement is enough.

112
00:06:43,610 --> 00:06:46,701
It's a bit like "good enough" in English.

113
00:06:46,701 --> 00:06:49,741
Right, but the tone depends on the situation.

114
00:06:49,741 --> 00:06:53,781
Sometimes it's relaxed, and sometimes it sounds careless.

115
00:06:53,781 --> 00:06:57,089
For home cooking, you can say the amount of salt just needs to be roughly right.

116
00:06:57,089 --> 00:07:02,671
Yes, because everyone has different tastes. You don't need to measure it to the exact gram.

117
00:07:02,671 --> 00:07:06,408
But can you say "close enough" when baking a cake?

118
00:07:06,408 --> 00:07:08,108
Not for some steps.

119
00:07:08,108 --> 00:07:13,556
If the flour, temperature, or time is far off, the cake will fail.

120
00:07:13,556 --> 00:07:16,702
That's why you told me to close the oven door just now.

121
00:07:16,702 --> 00:07:22,853
Right. When baking, chà bu duō can have very concrete consequences.

122
00:07:22,853 --> 00:07:26,008
Doctors definitely can't be approximate when prescribing medicine.

123
00:07:26,008 --> 00:07:33,091
Exactly. Numbers involving health, safety, money, and contracts all need to be accurate.

124
00:07:33,091 --> 00:07:35,822
Accurate means neither too much nor too little.

125
00:07:35,822 --> 00:07:40,559
Right, at least to the level of precision that the situation requires.

126
00:07:40,559 --> 00:07:42,992
Can the required level of precision change too?

127
00:07:42,992 --> 00:07:47,472
Of course. A friend being two minutes late is nothing,

128
00:07:47,472 --> 00:07:50,229
but two minutes late for a flight might mean the gate has closed.

129
00:07:50,229 --> 00:07:57,092
One extra slice of ginger in soup is fine, but a little extra in an experiment may matter.

130
00:07:57,092 --> 00:08:02,600
Very good. To decide whether chà bu duō is acceptable, first look at the consequences.

131
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:05,902
The more serious the consequences, the less you can settle for chà bu duō.

132
00:08:05,902 --> 00:08:09,069
That's a very practical rule.

133
00:08:09,069 --> 00:08:13,015
When someone says "close enough," are they lowering the standard?

134
00:08:13,015 --> 00:08:17,818
Sometimes. Other times they're just telling you not to chase perfection.

135
00:08:17,818 --> 00:08:20,125
Those feel very different.

136
00:08:20,125 --> 00:08:23,207
So you need to look at why they're saying it.

137
00:08:23,207 --> 00:08:26,527
When I'm designing a poster and my friend says,

138
00:08:26,527 --> 00:08:29,658
"It's good enough," they may be telling me not to keep editing until midnight.

139
00:08:29,658 --> 00:08:35,021
Right. They think it's already usable, and more changes won't make it much better.

140
00:08:35,021 --> 00:08:39,612
If my boss says "close enough" without even looking, I might worry.

141
00:08:39,612 --> 00:08:43,772
Because you don't know whether they're satisfied or just don't want to look carefully.

142
00:08:43,772 --> 00:08:47,347
How can I ask without sounding like I'm challenging them?

143
00:08:47,347 --> 00:08:51,355
You can ask, "Are there any parts that still need adjusting?"

144
00:08:51,355 --> 00:08:55,575
Making the question specific also makes it easier for them to answer.

145
00:08:55,575 --> 00:09:00,883
Exactly. Instead of repeatedly asking whether it's okay, ask which part needs changing.

146
00:09:00,883 --> 00:09:05,174
I've noticed chà bu duō often appears together with jiù xíng, "that's fine."

147
00:09:05,174 --> 00:09:09,034
Right. Chà bu duō jiù xíng means it doesn't need to be exactly the same

148
00:09:09,034 --> 00:09:10,904
or done perfectly.

149
00:09:10,904 --> 00:09:15,210
For example, "Just line up the tables roughly. That's fine."

150
00:09:15,210 --> 00:09:18,644
You could say that if you're only using them temporarily.

151
00:09:18,644 --> 00:09:22,719
But it might not be acceptable in a high-end restaurant.

152
00:09:22,719 --> 00:09:26,307
Right, because the standards there are higher.

153
00:09:26,307 --> 00:09:29,545
So chà bu duō isn't a phrase only lazy people use.

154
00:09:29,545 --> 00:09:31,345
Of course not.

155
00:09:31,345 --> 00:09:35,410
It can also help us spend time on more important things.

156
00:09:35,410 --> 00:09:37,842
That makes me think of perfectionism.

157
00:09:37,842 --> 00:09:42,428
If you want everything to be one hundred percent perfect, you may spend far too much time.

158
00:09:42,428 --> 00:09:46,431
The last five percent often takes half the time.

159
00:09:46,431 --> 00:09:51,863
Exactly. Knowing when "good enough" really is enough is also a skill.

160
00:09:51,863 --> 00:09:57,303
But if everything is only close enough, won't a person become more and more careless?

161
00:09:57,303 --> 00:10:04,037
They might. The point isn't to abandon standards, but to choose the right standard.

162
00:10:04,037 --> 00:10:07,978
Do important things carefully, and don't keep revising minor things forever.

163
00:10:07,978 --> 00:10:12,125
Right, and first make sure everyone understands the requirements.

164
00:10:12,125 --> 00:10:16,342
I have a Chinese friend whose catchphrase is chà bu duō.

165
00:10:16,342 --> 00:10:18,362
What does he say?

166
00:10:18,362 --> 00:10:21,402
Ask him what he wants to eat, and he says chà bu duō.

167
00:10:21,402 --> 00:10:24,306
Ask when he'll come, and he also says chà bu duō.

168
00:10:24,306 --> 00:10:27,759
"What to eat is chà bu duō" sounds incomplete.

169
00:10:27,759 --> 00:10:29,976
I suspect he means anything is fine.

170
00:10:29,976 --> 00:10:34,197
Then he should say, "They're all about the same," or "Anything is fine."

171
00:10:34,197 --> 00:10:39,003
"They're all about the same" means there isn't an obvious difference among the choices.

172
00:10:39,003 --> 00:10:44,008
Right. For example, the prices at these restaurants are all similar.

173
00:10:44,008 --> 00:10:46,988
If I say,

174
00:10:46,988 --> 00:10:50,461
"Anything to eat is about the same," it may mean there's nothing I especially want.

175
00:10:50,461 --> 00:10:54,838
Exactly. It may also mean you don't care much about the decision.

176
00:10:54,838 --> 00:10:57,413
Could the other person think I'm not interested?

177
00:10:57,413 --> 00:11:01,069
If you answer that way every time, they might.

178
00:11:01,069 --> 00:11:04,809
Then I can give some direction first, like, "Anything is fine,

179
00:11:04,809 --> 00:11:06,148
but I'd like something hot."

180
00:11:06,148 --> 00:11:11,227
Good. That keeps you flexible while giving the other person useful information.

181
00:11:11,227 --> 00:11:15,036
Mr. Almost is a famous character in China, right?

182
00:11:15,036 --> 00:11:18,415
Yes. He's a character written by Hu Shi.

183
00:11:18,415 --> 00:11:22,078
He thought being a little off didn't matter, no matter what he did.

184
00:11:22,078 --> 00:11:27,317
In the end, lots of small mistakes added up to a big problem.

185
00:11:27,317 --> 00:11:30,750
So a "close-enough culture" can sometimes be a criticism.

186
00:11:30,750 --> 00:11:34,843
Right. It means people aren't careful and don't follow standards.

187
00:11:34,843 --> 00:11:39,215
Some people say all Chinese people like chà bu duō. Is that fair?

188
00:11:39,215 --> 00:11:40,715
Of course not.

189
00:11:40,715 --> 00:11:44,722
You can't label everyone with one phrase.

190
00:11:44,722 --> 00:11:50,018
Every country has very precise people and people who are more casual.

191
00:11:50,018 --> 00:11:55,615
Exactly. Different jobs and situations also have different requirements.

192
00:11:55,615 --> 00:11:57,555
British people say "nearly there"

193
00:11:57,555 --> 00:11:59,995
when they're still very far away too.

194
00:11:59,995 --> 00:12:04,786
See? This isn't a problem with one particular language. It's a human problem.

195
00:12:04,786 --> 00:12:08,450
People all want to make bad news sound smaller.

196
00:12:08,450 --> 00:12:13,685
For example, a project is only seventy percent done, but someone says it's almost finished.

197
00:12:13,685 --> 00:12:17,487
Because seventy percent sounds like there's still a lot left.

198
00:12:17,487 --> 00:12:23,147
Right. So when you hear chà bu duō, you can gently ask a specific question.

199
00:12:23,147 --> 00:12:24,807
How much longer?

200
00:12:24,807 --> 00:12:26,672
What has been completed so far?

201
00:12:26,672 --> 00:12:33,052
Good. You're not obsessing over one phrase; you're confirming the facts.

202
00:12:33,052 --> 00:12:36,055
Should I do that when arranging a time too?

203
00:12:36,055 --> 00:12:39,359
If you genuinely need to plan, of course.

204
00:12:39,359 --> 00:12:45,579
If a friend says they'll arrive around six, I can ask whether they mean before or after six.

205
00:12:45,579 --> 00:12:51,176
Or simply say, "I need to leave at 6:10. Can you get here before then?"

206
00:12:51,176 --> 00:12:57,251
Stating my own limit feels more natural than demanding that the other person be exact.

207
00:12:57,251 --> 00:13:01,837
Right. It also lets them know why you need a specific time.

208
00:13:01,837 --> 00:13:06,928
If we're just casually meeting for coffee on the weekend, we don't need to be exact to the minute.

209
00:13:06,928 --> 00:13:11,734
Exactly. When the relationship is relaxed, the timing can have some flexibility too.

210
00:13:11,734 --> 00:13:16,255
Why does chà bu duō ba sound a little uncertain?

211
00:13:16,255 --> 00:13:18,915
Because ba softens the tone. It sounds

212
00:13:18,915 --> 00:13:22,919
like an estimate or like you don't want to be too definite.

213
00:13:22,919 --> 00:13:26,219
I ask whether these two cakes are equally sweet, and you say,

214
00:13:26,219 --> 00:13:27,493
"More or less, I guess."

215
00:13:27,493 --> 00:13:32,141
That means I think they're close, but I haven't compared them carefully.

216
00:13:32,141 --> 00:13:35,864
I ask whether the work is done, and you say, "Almost, I guess."

217
00:13:35,864 --> 00:13:38,366
Then it sounds even more like you aren't sure yourself.

218
00:13:38,366 --> 00:13:42,339
"Not sure" means you don't know whether you're right.

219
00:13:42,339 --> 00:13:46,687
Right. When trust matters, it's best not to give only that kind of answer.

220
00:13:46,687 --> 00:13:49,773
Chà bu duō le and chà bu duō ba are different too.

221
00:13:49,773 --> 00:13:56,082
Chà bu duō le often means something is almost complete, or it's time for the next step.

222
00:13:56,082 --> 00:13:59,749
For example, "Dinner is almost ready. Go wash your hands."

223
00:13:59,749 --> 00:14:03,840
Right. Or, "It's almost dark. Let's head back."

224
00:14:03,840 --> 00:14:08,142
Chà bu duō ba sounds more like an uncertain judgment.

225
00:14:08,142 --> 00:14:10,289
Exactly right.

226
00:14:10,289 --> 00:14:13,160
Is there also an expression chà bu liǎo duō shao?

227
00:14:13,160 --> 00:14:18,042
Yes. It means the difference between two things won't be very large.

228
00:14:18,042 --> 00:14:22,493
For example, taking the subway or bus won't make much difference in travel time.

229
00:14:22,493 --> 00:14:26,926
Right. Here you can't simply replace it with chà bu duō le.

230
00:14:26,926 --> 00:14:31,074
You can say the subway and bus take about the same amount of time.

231
00:14:31,074 --> 00:14:37,381
Yes. One stresses that the results are similar, and the other stresses that the gap is small.

232
00:14:37,381 --> 00:14:42,384
At this point in learning Chinese, it feels like every little word has its own job.

233
00:14:42,384 --> 00:14:46,267
You've started noticing the details. That's a good thing.

234
00:14:46,267 --> 00:14:48,771
Let's do a quick exercise.

235
00:14:48,771 --> 00:14:53,771
Okay. First question: two clothes have very similar colors,

236
00:14:53,771 --> 00:14:55,421
but they aren't exactly the same.

237
00:14:55,421 --> 00:14:58,936
I say, "These two clothes are about the same color."

238
00:14:58,936 --> 00:15:04,386
Good. Second: your report is ninety percent complete.

239
00:15:04,386 --> 00:15:09,336
I say, "The report is almost finished. I still need to check the last part."

240
00:15:09,336 --> 00:15:11,016
Very clear. Third:

241
00:15:11,016 --> 00:15:15,129
a friend asks when you went to bed last night.

242
00:15:15,129 --> 00:15:18,712
Around midnight, or midnight-ish.

243
00:15:18,712 --> 00:15:24,093
the instructions say to take two pills, and you aren't sure whether you can take three.

244
00:15:24,093 --> 00:15:28,241
I can't say "close enough." I should read the instructions or ask a doctor.

245
00:15:28,241 --> 00:15:33,693
you've changed an unimportant title twenty times.

246
00:15:33,693 --> 00:15:39,269
I can tell myself, "It's good enough. I'll do the more important work first."

247
00:15:39,269 --> 00:15:44,147
Full marks. Last question: your friend says they're almost there.

248
00:15:44,147 --> 00:15:46,721
I ask, "How many more minutes?"

249
00:15:46,721 --> 00:15:49,942
Looks like you won't be waiting foolishly at the door anymore.

250
00:15:49,942 --> 00:15:52,888
No, I still need to know which friend it is.

251
00:15:52,888 --> 00:15:54,908
Why?

252
00:15:54,908 --> 00:15:59,188
If it's the friend who made me wait twenty-five minutes,

253
00:15:59,188 --> 00:16:01,675
I'll go downstairs ten minutes late.

254
00:16:01,675 --> 00:16:06,729
Be careful. If both of you use chà bu duō, you may end up never meeting at all.

255
00:16:06,729 --> 00:16:11,823
Then I'd better say, "I'll wait at the door at six. Please try to be on time."

256
00:16:11,823 --> 00:16:14,058
That's much clearer.

257
00:16:14,058 --> 00:16:17,439
Ultimately, there's nothing wrong with chà bu duō itself.

258
00:16:17,439 --> 00:16:22,605
Right. The question is whether we use it in the right situation.

259
00:16:22,605 --> 00:16:26,053
You can use chà bu duō for things that are similar.

260
00:16:26,053 --> 00:16:29,921
You can also say chà bu duō le for something close to completion.

261
00:16:29,921 --> 00:16:33,784
You can use chà bu duō for approximate times and numbers.

262
00:16:33,784 --> 00:16:38,020
When the standard doesn't need to be very high, you can say, "Close enough is fine."

263
00:16:38,020 --> 00:16:42,660
But with health, safety, money, and important work,

264
00:16:42,660 --> 00:16:44,684
you need to be more accurate.

265
00:16:44,684 --> 00:16:49,630
When you need to collaborate, give the other person specific progress and timing too.

266
00:16:49,630 --> 00:16:54,795
If chà bu duō sounds unclear, ask one specific question.

267
00:16:54,795 --> 00:16:58,881
You don't need to get angry or guess. Just confirm it.

268
00:16:58,881 --> 00:17:02,760
By the way, how is that cake now?

269
00:17:02,760 --> 00:17:04,978
Now it really is ready.

270
00:17:04,978 --> 00:17:07,196
Not just almost ready?

271
00:17:07,196 --> 00:17:11,989
No. I've turned off the oven and checked that the middle is cooked.

272
00:17:11,989 --> 00:17:15,066
Great. Then we'll split it half and half.

273
00:17:15,066 --> 00:17:18,441
You cut it, but the two pieces need to be the same size.

274
00:17:18,441 --> 00:17:20,746
Close enough is fine, right?

275
00:17:20,746 --> 00:17:25,127
If your piece is smaller, I'll say close enough is fine.

276
00:17:25,127 --> 00:17:27,558
What if my piece is bigger?

277
00:17:27,558 --> 00:17:31,135
Then I'll suddenly become a very precise person.

278
00:17:31,135 --> 00:17:36,642
Now I finally understand that chà bu duō can also depend on who benefits.

279
00:17:36,642 --> 00:17:39,437
That's a very real discovery.

280
00:17:39,437 --> 00:17:43,077
Have you ever met someone who said "almost," but

281
00:17:43,077 --> 00:17:44,958
then kept you waiting a long time?

282
00:17:44,958 --> 00:17:50,970
Tell us in the comments which situation you use chà bu duō in most often.

283
00:17:50,970 --> 00:17:55,762
You can also write one example sentence for each of the four meanings we learned today.

284
00:17:55,762 --> 00:18:01,695
Thank you for joining us as we made this imprecise phrase a little clearer.

285
00:18:01,695 --> 00:18:07,555
Next time someone says chà bu duō, I'll first decide whether the situation requires accuracy.

286
00:18:07,555 --> 00:18:13,195
Now let's go eat the cake precisely. We'll listen to more natural Chinese together next time.

